In game development, you might use a similar approach to handle particle systems. Here's a simple C# example using a particle system for a particle effect:

```csharp
public class ParticleSystem
{
    private List<Particle> particles = new List<Particle>();

    public void Update()
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < particles.Count; i++)
        {
            Particle particle = particles[i];
            particle.Update();
            if (particle.IsDone())
            {
                particles.Remove(particle);
            }
        }
    }

    public void AddParticle(Vector3 position, Vector3 velocity)
    {
        particles.Add(new Particle(position, velocity));
    }
}

public class Particle
{
    public Vector3 Position;
    public Vector3 Velocity;
    private float lifeTime;
    private float timer;

    public Particle(Vector3 position, Vector3 velocity)
    {
        Position = position;
        Velocity = velocity;
        lifeTime = 1f;
        timer = 0f;
    }

    public void Update()
    {
        Position += Velocity * Time.deltaTime;
        timer += Time.deltaTime;
    }

    public bool IsDone()
    {
        return timer >= lifeTime;
    }
}
```

This script creates a simple particle system where you can add particles with a position and velocity. Each particle updates its position and checks if it's done (e.g., if it's been alive for a certain amount of time). This way, you can create particle effects like explosions or trails.

